WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
You're about to start lesson four in the
mini-course. Again, I'm Alex Lyon and you

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.069
haven't yet subscribed, I encourage you
to do so and check the rest of the

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:12.059
videos on the channel out. On this
particular video, like the last one, some

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:16.650
people don't like a couple of the tips
because, if you fall in this category, you

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.439
might feel like well that's a lot of
structure and I don't normally

00:00:19.439 --> 00:00:21.240
communicate that way.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.449
Well, that's true. Not everybody
communicates in a structure, orderly way.

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:28.989
But, it is one of the best ways to sound
more concise, clear, and confident.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:30.740
So give it a try.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:35.010
Check your self-talk and motivation. Why
is it that you don't want to communicate

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:38.760
this way? Because if you can get to the
bottom of your inner thoughts, then

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.740
you're much more likely to succeed and
communicate a clear fashion.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.460
So, let's jump right in.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.300
In our last lesson, we looked
at concise statements. In lesson four, we'll

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.160
look at organized thoughts. Concise tip
number four. Organize your thoughts into

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.420
clear points. This is, of course, one way
to remain concise but it also adds an

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:07.950
incredible amount of clarity and
confidence to your message. Messages

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:12.210
without a clear structure are very hard
to follow for your listeners. And long

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:16.560
lists dilute each item's value. You've
probably sat through presentations where

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.869
somebody had 20 items are on a list. And
there's really no impact there when you

00:01:21.869 --> 00:01:26.400
have that many items. Much better to
create fewer clear points. A clear

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.130
organization shows clear, confident
thinking. Here we have a messy desk and a

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.880
joke I pulled from a comic. If you think
this is bad, you should see the inside of

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:42.329
my head. It's actually looks a little bit
like my desk. And, I'm all for creativity.

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:45.960
However, once we're sharing our thoughts
with somebody else, once we're

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:51.060
communicating, we really have to stay
more structured so that they can follow

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:57.540
along with what we're saying in a clear
and understandable way. Richard Fineman

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.100
said it like this. "If you can't explain
it to a six-year-old, you really don't

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.930
understand it." And the idea here is that
if you really understand something, you

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:12.690
can explain it in a very clear and
structured way for your listeners.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:16.950
They talk about being able to give a
freshman lecture on a concept and if you

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:22.560
can't give a freshman level lecture, then
you don't yourself understand it. So this

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.760
structure will help us clarify our own
thoughts and help it land for other people.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:32.640
Structure provides a schema or a mental
framework that helps keeps thoughts

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.660
clear for you, helps your listeners
follow your point. It gives your message

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:44.730
cohesion or an overarching arc and helps
everybody remember. Strategy one is to

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:49.470
break messages into two to three chunks.
For example, let's say you're having a

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:53.520
conversation about getting healthy and
you want to share this information with

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.120
somebody else. Instead of just rambling,
you want to break it down into clear

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.770
points. For example, you could talk about
diet, exercise, and the importance of

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:06.540
working out with a friend. And underneath
each of those themes, or some people call

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.860
them buckets, you would give your detail.
So, if you're talking about diet, you

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:15.630
might talk about counting calories or
weight watchers, etc. Under exercise, the

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:19.350
different options, and under working out
with a friend how you would hold each

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:23.730
other a little accountable for staying
on track. I was talking to a friend of mine and,

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:28.140
it's a real life example, and I talked
about his retirement plan. He's retired

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:33.239
and he said, 401k, I have some rental income,
and a little bit of Social Security.

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:37.739
Instead of giving a long-winded,
disorganize, rambling answer, he was able

00:03:37.739 --> 00:03:41.910
to give me clear themes and then we
continue the discussion about each of

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:47.130
these three items. And he provided some
nice detail. I was watching a movie about

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:52.530
a disabled man from Ghana many years ago. And he had one good leg and one disabled leg.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:56.489
And he finally got a meeting with a
community leader. And the leader said

00:03:56.489 --> 00:04:01.290
what are your priorities? And he said, I
want to help provide independence,

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:08.910
mobility, and sports for the disabled
people in Ghana. He had just a moment to

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.660
break the ice with an extremely clear
message, and to this day, I remember this

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:17.880
documentary film. He communicated in that clear and confident way. And I instantly

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.109
thought, Wow. This guy really knows what
he's talking about. When we do this, we

00:04:22.109 --> 00:04:26.250
want to organize our chunks, our main
points, into some logical

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:30.330
pattern. A nice logical pattern is going
to help people remember and it's going

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:35.220
to clarify our thoughts for ourselves.
Before-after, problem-solution-benefit,

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:41.040
cause-and-effect, steps in a sequence or
in other words chronological, etc. Lots of

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.400
different options here. You're not
limited to this list but it's nice to

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.480
have a list handy. For example, let's
practice with this stream of thought

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:54.690
example about somebody's day. I got up
got ready a breakfast and worked out. I

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:58.020
did some shopping on the way home from the gym. I bumped into Katy at the store,

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:01.740
she wants to get together tonight. I
actually got some work done during the

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:06.690
day, shot some videos, did some editing, and organized the tools in garage. I was

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:10.020
hoping to get together with Katy and
John tonight but I also have some people

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:14.100
to get back to, some calls to make, at
least a couple of texts to stay on top

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.810
of some things. So, this sounds a little
bit like rambling but you can basically

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:19.860
follow it.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:24.840
It's much more clear, however, if we
structure this into main points. For

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:29.460
example, chronologically. Morning,
afternoon, and evening. And each of those

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.030
items has a few details underneath it.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:38.280
Those themes provide a really great way
to clarify your message, make it much

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:42.990
more concise, and it'll sound very
confident compared to a rambling

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:47.430
disorganized message. It's handy to have
a list like this around when you're

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:50.669
preparing for meetings, important
conversations, and certainly

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:55.140
presentations because you don't have to
create this from scratch. You can invent

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:58.710
new ways but there are lots of great
scheme is already available for

00:05:58.710 --> 00:05:59.970
structuring your ideas.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:04.650
Strategy number two is to then state
your road map. So you have your structure

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:10.590
and now you want to forecast those
points aloud with key words only. So a

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:12.150
physician might say to you,

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:17.160
"Let's talk about symptoms and then
treatments." Now you have a roadmap for

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:22.290
how that conversation is going to go. And
I recommend also calling out your points

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:25.350
along the way. So, let's say you start
with symptoms. You talk about that for a

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:29.250
few minutes and if you are the physician
then you might say, "Now let's turn to your

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:34.020
treatment options." And that'll be a
really clear signal that you're shifting

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:39.280
gears and it again reminds people of that
schema, reminds them of your buckets, and

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:45.220
helps your message state clear. So
structure provides freedom even though

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:49.600
it seems like it boxes people in. We go
back to mindset that we talked about in

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:54.490
the earlier lesson on long-windedness. 
Why are we long-winded? Sometimes people resist

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:59.680
applying structure because it feels too
restrictive. It feels like it's robbing

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:04.060
them of something. But there are a lot of
benefits and there are freedoms that

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:07.690
you can experience within a good
structure. First of all it eliminates

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:11.470
tangents, which is good for your thinking,
and it's good for listeners. They don't

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:15.190
want to hear tangents anymore than you
want to get off on them. But it also

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:19.810
provides a place for more details. So if
you have clear main points, then you can

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:23.710
find some nice details and sort them
into the relevant place and then they'll

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:28.090
make sense. And it gives even long
stories a sense of cohesion. So let's say

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:31.720
you tell a five-minute story that's
disorganized. It's going to feel like it

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:36.760
lasts forever. If you tell a five-minute
story with a clear beginning, middle, and

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:41.260
end, then everybody will be able to
follow it and it won't feel long at all. So

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:45.880
structure is a tool to help you sort
need-to-know information versus nice-to-

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:50.500
know information. We talked about this in
an earlier lesson. If it doesn't fit into

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:54.910
your structure, either you have the wrong
structure, the wrong main points, or it's

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:58.390
just not need-to-know information. It
might be just something that's nice to

00:07:58.390 --> 00:08:02.200
know, which is why it doesn't fit in. And
that way when you communicate with

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:08.890
listeners, you're giving them high-value
information with every sentence. So we

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:12.370
want to organize our thoughts into clear
points. Our take-away strategies are to

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:17.350
break ideas into two to three chunks and
then state your roadmap really clearly

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:21.340
before you share your message and then
remind people of your points as you move

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:26.440
through them. So our task is to apply
this in conversations, meetings, and

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:31.240
presentations that you have coming up in
the coming days and I look forward to

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:34.560
seeing in the next lesson on effective
pauses.

